Now I understand the issue. Let me trace the problem:

1. When an option has a string default and an alias, the issue is about type preservation.
2. Looking at the defaults handling code:

```javascript
if (defaults) {
    for (k in opts.default) {
        opts.alias[k] = opts.alias[k] || [];
        (opts[typeof opts.default[k]] || []).push(k);
    }
}
```

This adds the key `k` to `opts.string` if `opts.default[k]` is a string. But it only adds the primary key, not the aliases.

3. Looking at the string/boolean propagation code:

```javascript
opts.string.forEach(key => {
    opts.string = opts.string.concat(opts.alias[key] = opts.alias[key] || []);
});

opts.boolean.forEach(key => {
    opts.boolean = opts.boolean.concat(opts.alias[key] = opts.alias[key] || []);
});
```

This runs BEFORE the defaults processing code. So when defaults are processed later, the aliases aren't added to the string/boolean arrays.

4. At the end, aliases are copied:

```javascript
if (alibi) {
    for (k in out) {
        arr = opts.alias[k] || [];
        while (arr.length > 0) {
            out[arr.shift()] = out[k];
        }
    }
